WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.950
Telemetry is security-relevant data collected from systems, applications, devices,

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:08.330
networks, identities, cloud environments, and security tools.

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:12.120
It records observable activity such as a user signing in, a process starting,

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:15.580
an application returning an error, a firewall allowing a connection,

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:17.920
or a cloud administrator changing a resource.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:21.640
Security teams use telemetry to understand what is happening across an environment.

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:25.980
A single event rarely tells the complete story, but related events can reveal sequences,

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:26.760
relationships,

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:31.040
and changes that help analysts distinguish normal operations from activity that deserves

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:31.990
investigation.

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:34.545
Telemetry is not one product or one log format.

00:00:34.545 --> 00:00:38.390
It includes events, alerts, metrics, traces, flow records, audit logs,

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:39.790
and contextual information.

00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:42.980
The value comes from collecting the right data, preserving useful details,

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:45.880
and making that information available when defenders need it.

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:50.380
In security operations, telemetry supports detection, investigation, threat hunting,

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:53.200
incident response, reporting, and continuous improvement.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:56.940
It is the evidence layer that allows teams to ask informed questions about identities,

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:00.170
assets, applications, communications, and changes over time.

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:02.650
Telemetry should also be distinguished from an alert.

00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:04.370
Telemetry is the underlying evidence,

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:08.260
while an alert is a conclusion or signal produced by evaluating some of that evidence.

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:12.480
Analysts may return to the original events to verify the alert, add context,

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:13.840
test another explanation,

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:17.440
or discover activity that the original analytic did not consider.
